# 12. TIME PROTECTION AND HUMAN WELLBEING

HatBridge must not optimise a person into exhaustion.

Create a Time Protection feature.

Users can mark:

- Family time.
- Rest.
- Personal appointments.
- Exercise.
- Learning.
- Recovery.
- Unavailable periods.

The system should recognise protected time when proposing schedules.

It should not automatically fill every empty calendar slot.

Provide a gentle workload overview.

Warn the user when the planned day appears overloaded.

Do not use guilt, streak pressure or manipulative productivity language.

The product's success metric is not the number of tasks completed.

It is whether users can make meaningful progress while preserving attention, autonomy and personal life.

